---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import GatewayCard from '@/components/ia/GatewayCard.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Use and tooling"
  description="Bind namespaces, query and validate data, download Turtle artefacts, and compare documentation renderings for the PDTF 1.0-derived ontology."
>
  <PageMeta category="PDTF 1.0 · extracted ontology" variant="brand" updated="Draft · under review" />
  <h1>Use and tooling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This is the practical route for consuming the extracted corpus. It brings together the
    namespace contract, queries, validation recipe, content-negotiation guidance, downloadable
    Turtle and the documentation-tool comparison used to assemble the human-readable reference.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">PDTF 1.0-derived draft</p>
    <p>
      Stable routes and downloadable artefacts support inspection and testing; they do not make
      this an approved SPDTF 2.0 ontology. Pin the corpus version you tested, follow the documented
      validator settings, and carry the draft status into downstream use.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="workflow">Consumer workflow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Resolve namespaces.</strong> Bind the kind-split term, scheme, shape, graph and harness namespaces without collapsing their resource kinds.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose an artefact.</strong> Download the class, shape or merged Turtle appropriate to the task and record the version used.</li>
    <li><strong>Query or dereference.</strong> Use the sample SPARQL and stable resource IRIs to inspect the committed model.</li>
    <li><strong>Validate deliberately.</strong> Use the documented SHACL configuration, including advanced rules where required, and compare results with known caveats.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat tool output as a view.</strong> Generated renderings help inspect layers; none independently defines the ontology’s authority.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="start">Choose a practical route</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/usage" title="Consume the ontology" eyebrow="Queries and downloads" description="Get the prefix map, runnable SPARQL, content-negotiation guidance, the pyshacl advanced=True recipe and links to the downloadable Turtle bundles." />
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/namespaces" title="Namespaces and prefixes" eyebrow="IRI contract" description="Inspect the kind-split namespace scheme, external vocabulary bindings, collision-avoidance rationale and example IRIs." />
    <GatewayCard href="/ontology/bake-off" title="Tool bake-off and scorecard" eyebrow="Documentation tooling" description="Compare every documentation tool against the full corpus, its per-layer strengths and omissions, and the M6 rubric used to score and select the woven reference’s presentation methods." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="representation">Source, representation and tool are different things</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">Use it for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Committed Turtle corpus</th><td>Machine-readable source for this extracted draft</td><td>Loading, querying, validation and reproducible generation.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Native ontology pages</th><td>Task-oriented and dereferenceable documentation</td><td>Interpretation, discovery, relationships, constraints and provenance.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Third-party tool renderings</th><td>Supplementary views of selected layers</td><td>Comparison and specialist inspection, subject to each tool’s omissions.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Check <a href="/ontology/trust-and-governance">limitations and known issues</a> before
    operational use, and use <a href="/ontology/terms-and-model-resources">Terms and model resources</a>
    when you need to resolve a particular class, property, shape, concept or scheme.
  </p>
</Layout>
